  "query_preview": "..\n\nAnother outstanding mid October day is in progress across the Great \nLakes courtesy of Ohio valley high pressure and a dry cold front \nentering the upper Midwest. SW wind gusting 20 to 30 mph between \nthese systems, with a full sun assist, is lifting temperatures \ntoward mid 60s highs, a few degrees above normal for Oct 18. Mild \nand dry air remain inbound this evening until gusts decouple around \nsunset. A little more wind toward the Ohio border and a little less \ntoward the Tri Cities, compared to last night, create a more uniform \nfield of low temperatures mainly in the upper 30s to lower 40s. \nThere is a chance for patchy frost in wind sheltered areas through \nsunrise Monday. \n\nMore of the same pleasant fall weather is on tap during Monday \nthrough mid week as a maturing split flow upper air configuration \nfavors a ridge centered on the Midwest and Great Lakes. The northern \nstream Hudson Bay low also leaves the Midwest cold front to \ndissipate in the northern Lakes in favor of reinforced high pressure \nthrough Tuesday. Extension of the warming trend is the weather \nhighlight with strong support across model guidance. Highs in the \nmid to upper 60s are only held in check by weaker SW flow/warm \nadvection Monday shifting to light easterly Tuesday. \n\nThe warming trend peaks Wednesday as highs near 70 are reachable in \na low level wind field that returns SW ahead of the next northern \nstream cold front. This front approaches the northern Great Lakes \nWednesday night and has a chance to settle into southern Lower Mi \nThursday. Model consensus illustrates a moisture-starved boundary \ncapable of a cloud increase but minimal potential for rain before \nthe front dissipates in favor of slightly cooler high pressure \nheading into next weekend.",
